WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.903 --> 00:00:10.573
imece runs a team of Claude Code agents on one codebase, on one screen.

2
00:00:10.573 --> 00:00:17.121
A leader reads your checkout and hires workers as the work needs them; each worker

3
00:00:17.121 --> 00:00:21.514
changes a git worktree of its own, never your checkout.

4
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:24.721
This is Home: one list.

5
00:00:24.721 --> 00:00:31.594
What is to decide comes first, grouped by what deciding costs you: permissions,

6
00:00:31.594 --> 00:00:33.247
questions, changes.

7
00:00:33.247 --> 00:00:36.727
Then who is at work, project by project.

8
00:00:36.727 --> 00:00:44.035
A worker's row is its card, the step of its own to-do list it is on, and dimmer, the

9
00:00:44.035 --> 00:00:51.343
tool call it is making; the mark in front says where it stands: at work, waiting for

10
00:00:51.343 --> 00:00:52.822
you, done, stuck.

11
00:00:52.822 --> 00:00:59.956
Capital J opens the journal in a window: what happened, card by card, and whom you

12
00:00:59.956 --> 00:01:00.652
hold up.

13
00:01:01.852 --> 00:01:10.328
Enter looks deeper: an agent's detail, with how full its context is, its to-do list

14
00:01:10.328 --> 00:01:18.397
step by step, and its card's story; tab turns to its diff and its conversation.

15
00:01:18.397 --> 00:01:24.218
Enter again shows the raw stream from Claude Code itself.

16
00:01:24.218 --> 00:01:25.954
Escape goes back.

17
00:01:25.954 --> 00:01:33.103
Three depths, everywhere: the list, one piece of work, the raw stream.

18
00:01:34.304 --> 00:01:40.664
Agents talk to each other over a bus: they ask, answer, hand work off, report

19
00:01:40.664 --> 00:01:43.969
progress, and say when they are blocked.

20
00:01:43.969 --> 00:01:49.008
imece reads only the envelope; the body is never interpreted.

21
00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:54.955
Let us speed the simulation up, let them work for a while, and pause it.

22
00:01:56.591 --> 00:02:01.041
Everything that needs you is at the top of Home: TO DECIDE.

23
00:02:01.041 --> 00:02:07.303
A change a worker has finished, a command Claude Code would have asked you about, a

24
00:02:07.303 --> 00:02:08.585
question for you.

25
00:02:08.585 --> 00:02:14.847
Each row says the whole ask and ends with the keys that decide it; g d jumps to the

26
00:02:14.847 --> 00:02:15.299
first.

27
00:02:15.299 --> 00:02:20.882
The terminal's title counts them, so a tab in the background shows it too.

28
00:02:22.082 --> 00:02:29.309
g c goes to the first change waiting, under the commands and questions, and enter

29
00:02:29.309 --> 00:02:30.112
opens it.

30
00:02:30.112 --> 00:02:37.250
Its first line is the verdict: what ran on its tree, whether it stays inside its

31
00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:42.067
card's paths, whether it can be approved as it stands.

32
00:02:42.067 --> 00:02:49.027
Then what the worker's worktree holds since its last done: the files above the

33
00:02:49.027 --> 00:02:51.525
patch, then the diff itself.

34
00:02:51.525 --> 00:02:56.253
j and k scroll it; the brackets go from file to file.

35
00:02:57.454 --> 00:03:04.427
a applies the change to your checkout, staged, for you to look over and commit, and

36
00:03:04.427 --> 00:03:06.947
Home moves on to the next row.

37
00:03:06.947 --> 00:03:11.568
r sends it back and opens the composer, so you say why.

38
00:03:11.568 --> 00:03:18.373
Git does its work on a thread of its own; the journal says when the change is in.

39
00:03:18.373 --> 00:03:22.825
Your checkout is the one place an agent never writes.

40
00:03:24.026 --> 00:03:26.586
Let the simulation run again.

41
00:03:26.586 --> 00:03:33.822
The header has four things: the projects, what is to decide, one pot for the whole

42
00:03:33.822 --> 00:03:37.176
team, kept between runs, and the time.

43
00:03:37.176 --> 00:03:42.559
The pot's rate and how long it lasts are a colon budget away.

44
00:03:42.559 --> 00:03:47.678
On a narrow screen, the least wanted parts give way first.

45
00:03:48.878 --> 00:03:55.177
The board, the code map and the timeline are windows over Home.

46
00:03:55.177 --> 00:04:03.476
Colon board: the cards the leaders posted, who claimed which, what is done; an idle

47
00:04:03.476 --> 00:04:06.975
worker may claim a card on its own.

48
00:04:06.975 --> 00:04:12.774
Colon code: one row per file, and who holds which symbols.

49
00:04:12.774 --> 00:04:21.172
Colon timeline: one lane per agent over the last hour, each column what it did then:

50
00:04:21.172 --> 00:04:28.171
writing, reading, thinking, asking a teammate, waiting for you, stuck.

51
00:04:28.171 --> 00:04:34.870
The brackets make a column five seconds, half a minute or a minute.

52
00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:37.169
Escape closes a window.

53
00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:41.694
You talk to the leader, not to thirty agents.

54
00:04:41.694 --> 00:04:47.750
Type what you want; the leader turns it into cards, and the workers take them from

55
00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:48.489
the board.

56
00:04:48.489 --> 00:04:52.181
The reply comes back as something waiting for you.

57
00:04:53.382 --> 00:05:01.755
q asks before quitting: how many agents are mid-turn, how many changes you have not

58
00:05:01.755 --> 00:05:02.663
reviewed.

59
00:05:02.663 --> 00:05:03.975
s lists them.

60
00:05:03.975 --> 00:05:12.348
Everything imece knows is kept in one SQLite file; the next imece opens on the same

61
00:05:12.348 --> 00:05:17.393
screen, every agent asleep until you write to one.

62
00:05:18.593 --> 00:05:20.265
imece is open source.

63
00:05:20.265 --> 00:05:24.884
Install it with one line, and try the simulation for free.

